@page "/Clipboard"
@using BlazorDatasheet.SharedPages.Components.Examples.Data

<h3>Copy, cut and paste</h3>

<div class="block">
    The datasheet copies and pastes the way a spreadsheet does. Within a workbook a paste carries values, formulas,
    formatting, merged cells and cell types; relative formula references shift with the paste and <code>$</code>
    references stay put. A copied block tiles across a selection that is an exact multiple of its size.
</div>

<table class="table">
    <thead>
    <tr>
        <th>Shortcut</th>
        <th>Action</th>
    </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
    <tr>
        <td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>C</kbd></td>
        <td>Copy the selection. The cells get a moving dashed border while they are on the clipboard.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>X</kbd></td>
        <td>Cut the selection. Nothing changes until it is pasted.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd></td>
        <td>Paste everything at the selection.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>Shift</kbd>+<kbd>V</kbd></td>
        <td>Paste values only: formulas are pasted as their results, and formatting is left alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td><kbd>Esc</kbd></td>
        <td>End copy mode. Starting to type into a cell, or changing the copied cells, ends it too.</td>
    </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<div class="block">
    The right-click menu offers Copy, Cut and Paste, plus a Paste special sub-menu with Paste values and Paste
    formats. Pasting from the menu reads the system clipboard, so the browser may ask the user to allow it; when it
    refuses, the workbook's own clipboard is pasted instead. Use <code>SheetMenuOptions</code> to hide these items:
    <code>CopyEnabled</code>, <code>CutEnabled</code>, <code>PasteEnabled</code> and <code>PasteSpecialEnabled</code>.
</div>

<h4>Cut moves cells</h4>

<div class="block">
    Pasting a cut block moves it, as in Excel. The source is cleared, formulas inside the block keep pointing at the
    same cells, and formulas anywhere in the workbook that refer to cells inside the block are rewritten to the new
    location. References that only partly overlap the block are left alone. The whole move is one undo step.
</div>

<h4>Other applications</h4>

<div class="block">
    A copy also goes to the system clipboard as tab-delimited text and as an HTML table with inline styles, so Excel,
    Google Sheets and other BlazorDatasheet instances paste it with its formatting and merges. Pasting from those
    applications brings in values, bold/italic/underline, colours, alignment, borders and merged cells. Plain text is
    split on tabs and line breaks, with Excel-style quoting for cells that contain them.
</div>

<div class="block">
    The HTML table is marked with the id of the workbook clipboard entry (<code>data-bds-clipboard-id</code>), which is
    how a paste knows to use the live cells rather than the HTML. Formulas, typed values and cell types travel in
    <code>data-bds-*</code> attributes so another BlazorDatasheet can paste them exactly. The clipboard API needs a
    secure context (HTTPS or localhost); without one the in-app clipboard still works.
</div>

<h4>Programmatic access</h4>

<div class="block">
    <code>Workbook.Clipboard</code> holds the current entry and has <code>Copy</code>, <code>Cut</code>,
    <code>Paste</code>, <code>PasteSnapshot</code>, <code>PasteText</code> and <code>Clear</code>. Its
    <code>BeforePaste</code> event is cancellable and lets you change the paste mode; <code>Changed</code> and
    <code>Pasted</code> report what happened. <code>Datasheet.PasteFromClipboardAsync</code> reads the system
    clipboard through the browser.
</div>

